1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,880
 Netscape is of course known for its Netscape Navigator product, first commercial browser

2
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:11,720
 on the web, so first tool, first generation really used to get on the web. And that was

3
00:00:11,720 --> 00:00:18,600
 successful for a while, and then competition became quite stiff from Microsoft. The different

4
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:24,420
 way to compete would be by building a shared asset, set of Netscape versus Microsoft that

5
00:00:24,420 --> 00:00:31,280
 Netscape would gather contributions of volunteers and other commercial partners, and build a

6
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:37,260
 product that would be shared. And that group of people knew that to be open source you

7
00:00:37,260 --> 00:00:43,080
 had to be real. You couldn't just say, "Oh, we're open source, love us." You really had

8
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:48,360
 to manage it differently. So at the time there were six or seven or eight of us who were

9
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:54,160
 employed by Netscape as Mozilla.org staff, and another 100 or 150 employed by Netscape

10
00:00:54,160 --> 00:01:00,760
 as Netscape engineers building the Netscape product, and contributing their work to the

11
00:01:00,760 --> 00:01:05,960
 Mozilla open source project. So we were all in the same buildings, we all went to many

12
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:11,480
 of the same meetings, but we had slightly different goals. Because although we call

13
00:01:11,480 --> 00:01:18,900
 it the Netscape product, Netscape had been purchased by AOL already. And so the client

14
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:24,180
 itself was diminishing in importance, and the importance of the Netscape client was

15
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:31,600
 to push traffic to the AOL websites. But we, of course, at Mozilla.org were an anomaly,

16
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:36,800
 because our charter was to build a successful open source project. And so that worked for

17
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:45,180
 quite a while. There were tensions. These were the dark years for Mozilla. We came to

18
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:51,560
 understand we needed to rebuild our core technology. That took a long time. While we were doing

19
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:56,400
 that, market share continued to slide. And there was a set of tensions over what does

20
00:01:56,400 --> 00:02:02,800
 it mean to run a real open source project? And as it turned out, there were two competing

21
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:08,720
 views. And one of you was that open source is phenomenal, we're very happy that the

22
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:13,400
 code is out there, anyone is very welcome to take that code and use it. We absolutely

23
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:20,600
 want to build a large and engaged contributor community, but all decisions and all authority

24
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:25,960
 will be held by the managed group and we'll make those decisions as we think best, which

25
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:32,640
 is, of course, to benefit the client we're trying to build, which was the Netscape browser.

26
00:02:32,640 --> 00:02:41,720
 We at Mozilla.org were convinced it wouldn't be successful, that building a product to

27
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:47,760
 benefit AOL only wouldn't generate the kind of interest, either from individual volunteers

28
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:53,000
 or from commercial partners, that we needed to be successful, and that we found the difference

29
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:59,120
 in perspective from the volunteers made the quality of the project much higher. They were

30
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:05,440
 under immense pressure to ship this product because the company needed it. And not only

31
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:11,400
 was it difficult technically and in an engineering sense, but we at Mozilla.org were saying,

32
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:14,960
 and there's these other things, you may think it's good enough, but we're telling you it's

33
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:20,320
 not because this is the feedback that we're getting and you need to change some of the

34
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:27,080
 development processes and management practices. And so they thought, well, I'm not sure what

35
00:03:27,080 --> 00:03:30,840
 they thought, but we experienced it as, no, we really need to keep going on the way that

36
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:37,840
 we're going until we get this product shipped. But unfortunately, the Mozilla community was

37
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:44,360
 correct because when that product shipped, it was a failure. That was Netscape 6. Universally

38
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:49,840
 acknowledged as a bad product and pretty universally acknowledged as the end of the Netscape product

39
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:54,840
 line. That all those people that had held on and hoped through the Netscape four days

40
00:03:54,840 --> 00:04:00,840
 and waited and waited and waited for the update once they got it pretty much gave up. So that's

41
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:07,600
 what we experienced. So that certainly didn't help us. That was unfortunate. It did reinforce

42
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:17,240
 our view that our view of the world wasn't crazy. And so we continued on and it was still

43
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:24,520
 long and slow to get technology and products that we liked. And the tensions continued.

44
00:04:24,520 --> 00:04:29,160
 The management tensions continued. The failure of Netscape 6 didn't make anything easier.

45
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:35,420
 And certainly for the AOL folks, it really didn't make things easier either. And we fought

46
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:42,600
 a lot about the UI. I think UI is a constant source of tension. But in our case, it was

47
00:04:42,600 --> 00:04:48,840
 worse because some of these fights would be it makes sense to AOL to put something in

48
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:53,480
 the product, in the interface of the product. Either a button to an AOL site or something

49
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:58,920
 that has an ad in it or some feature that a partner has paid for so it generates revenue.

50
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:04,520
 We think it might send people to our site. Some of our users might really like it. So

51
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,920
 it all made sense from their point of view. But if you're not building the AOL client

52
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:12,920
 to benefit AOL and you're trying to build an open source product that is used for many

53
00:05:12,920 --> 00:05:17,960
 people, then of course those features are a big negative. And so those were very fierce

54
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:27,040
 fights because the management of the client group had very clear opportunities in hand

55
00:05:27,040 --> 00:05:33,260
 that they wanted to build into their product. And we were this small group saying no.

56
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:38,840
 We're pretty opposed in that case. And we would say no, you can't put it into the core

57
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:42,720
 product. You're welcome to have a build system on your own and add it in later. And that's

58
00:05:42,720 --> 00:05:46,720
 how we ended up doing this thing. So we did develop a system for it, but of course it's

59
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:52,160
 awkward and you're always rubbing against what should go where. And it turned out even

60
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:58,260
 in the very early days that the open source Mozilla versions of the product got a lot

61
00:05:58,260 --> 00:06:05,320
 more testing than the Netscape versions. And we also tried to build in some quality mechanisms

62
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:12,300
 that again seem normal today. So you get hired as an employee, you don't automatically get

63
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:19,180
 the right to check your code into the tree. But someone has to look at your qualifications

64
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:26,280
 and say Mozilla and the way we work, we understand your coding level, we understand you know

65
00:06:26,280 --> 00:06:29,820
 how we work, we understand if something goes wrong with your code you know where you need

66
00:06:29,820 --> 00:06:33,800
 to be and all of these things. And that's a very difficult setting for a hiring manager

67
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:39,040
 to say well I've just hired you and you can't be as efficient as you think you can because

68
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:44,880
 there's yet another vetting process by this other group. So I understand why it was difficult,

69
00:06:44,880 --> 00:06:49,400
 but on the other hand as an open source project if you can't control the quality of the code

70
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:56,000
 and you don't have any say in it you're not very real. Well client fortunes declined

71
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:03,160
 and the Netscape browser declined and declined precipitously after the Netscape 6. And so

72
00:07:03,160 --> 00:07:11,960
 AOL was interested in laying off people and in one of the big layoffs I was included.

73
00:07:11,960 --> 00:07:22,100
 And that was seen, that was in 2001 and that was seen as a power struggle as well because

74
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:29,060
 by that time the fights back and forth about what we were building and who was making decisions

75
00:07:29,060 --> 00:07:33,860
 were pretty well known within the engineering organization. It's hard for them not to be.

76
00:07:33,860 --> 00:07:37,420
 I mean it's true anywhere right, but in some cases the fights and the bugs are all very

77
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:49,460
 public as well. And so I was laid off or fired depending on how you want to describe it.

78
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:56,840
 And as it turned out it wasn't really possible to take my place. And I continued as a volunteer

79
00:07:56,840 --> 00:08:01,700
 and the Netscape engineering organization was very clear whose leadership they were

80
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:10,060
 most interested in following. And so we had to work out a way in which the management

81
00:08:10,060 --> 00:08:16,980
 at Netscape AOL that remained and I worked out a sort of a way to work together enough

82
00:08:16,980 --> 00:08:22,040
 so that they could ship the product they wanted to ship but that I continued to lead the project.

83
00:08:22,040 --> 00:08:32,020
 And I think that was a surprise, not so much to me I think. And so I worked as a volunteer

84
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:36,420
 on the Mozilla project for a number of years including the years in which we shipped our

85
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:42,500
 first product which we called Mozilla and which we were very proud of technically. And

86
00:08:42,500 --> 00:08:47,340
 for its day many people were surprised at how good it was, but it didn't have a good

87
00:08:47,340 --> 00:08:52,580
 user experience. But eventually I ended up working on another open source project with

88
00:08:52,580 --> 00:09:00,060
 Mitch Kapoor for a while and that was lucky. He had started this open project and reached

89
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:06,120
 out to talk to Brendan and I about it to learn something. And the day that he was scheduled

90
00:09:06,120 --> 00:09:11,380
 to come down and talk with us was the day that I was laid off. And so it was an odd

91
00:09:11,380 --> 00:09:17,660
 thing of timing. Yeah that was pre-Chandler but it was that. So I worked on that for a

92
00:09:17,660 --> 00:09:22,860
 while and Mitch was always a supporter of Mozilla. Even before Firefox when he didn't

93
00:09:22,860 --> 00:09:28,340
 like the product but he recognized that we were an important part of the ecosystem. And

94
00:09:28,340 --> 00:09:38,500
 that went on until 2003 when AOL decided to stop investing in the client almost completely.

95
00:09:38,500 --> 00:09:43,980
 And fortunately they knew that just killing it wasn't good. They knew enough about Mozilla

96
00:09:43,980 --> 00:09:54,480
 and the name and the brand to think it would be good to do something with it. And so eventually

97
00:09:54,480 --> 00:10:01,180
 I ended up working with them and some of the people there knew Mitch as well. And I was

98
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:05,860
 working with Mitch and they knew him and so we all spent a chunk of time trying to figure

99
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:13,320
 out what was possible. And my partner Brendan was still at Netscape, very eager to make

100
00:10:13,320 --> 00:10:19,660
 a move and many of the key people we wanted were also desperate to keep working on Mozilla.

101
00:10:19,660 --> 00:10:27,580
 And so we ended up getting a two million dollar seed money from AOL and Mitch was helpful

102
00:10:27,580 --> 00:10:33,420
 with that and a few other things. The trademark name Mozilla and the four giant servers which

103
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:38,340
 were so important to us at the time and which had taken us almost 18 months to get through

104
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:43,940
 the purchase cycle at AOL. And so I think we still have those boxes sitting around because

105
00:10:43,940 --> 00:10:49,040
 they were so important to us at the time. And so in 2003 the Mozilla Foundation was

106
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:56,460
 formed and Mitch was the first chairman because he had done so much and had so much to offer.

107
00:10:56,460 --> 00:11:04,820
 And Brian Balendorf and Chris Blizzard who worked on Mozilla forever and Brendan and

108
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:12,260
 I were on the board. And AOL understood that there were three or four people that would

109
00:11:12,260 --> 00:11:17,660
 be leaving when they did some kind of re-org when they closed down the client group and

110
00:11:17,660 --> 00:11:21,780
 that those people would be coming to join us. And of all the people that we thought would

111
00:11:21,780 --> 00:11:27,580
 be most helpful at Mozilla, they all came. That was about nine or ten. So we were and

112
00:11:27,580 --> 00:11:32,700
 Mitch continued to support me and one other person in Mozilla part time. So we were maybe

113
00:11:32,700 --> 00:11:39,700
 ten or eleven people. And Brendan and I had always felt that we needed employees. It was

114
00:11:39,700 --> 00:11:45,060
 exciting. It was a little scary because we knew that two million dollars wouldn't go

115
00:11:45,060 --> 00:11:49,940
 that far and that we had a lot of work to do to make ourselves real. We were still 18

116
00:11:49,940 --> 00:11:56,180
 months away from shipping, 15 months away from shipping Firefox. So that part was scary

117
00:11:56,180 --> 00:12:00,380
 but it was immensely exciting. One of the friends of Mozilla showed up and they had

118
00:12:00,380 --> 00:12:05,360
 a big lease and a little bit of space they could sublease to us. So we found a funky

119
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:12,920
 little room way in the back. Not even a sink. Very odd but we were really happy with it.

120
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:17,960
 And we worked away on building our product. At that time we made what was a pretty fundamental

121
00:12:17,960 --> 00:12:25,060
 change that was critical and we decided unambiguously to be a consumer product. That seems obvious

122
00:12:25,060 --> 00:12:28,540
 but when you're a bunch of developers it's not that easy. And it means that you have

123
00:12:28,540 --> 00:12:33,620
 to strip out a lot of the things that are clunky for a general consumer. You really

124
00:12:33,620 --> 00:12:38,860
 have to be determined that you're building for a general consumer, not yourself. And

125
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:43,060
 I think many open source projects probably don't want to do that and shouldn't try if

126
00:12:43,060 --> 00:12:46,980
 you don't want to because you have to keep at it. So we started doing that. Out of the

127
00:12:46,980 --> 00:12:53,620
 blue some visual designers appeared from Prince Edward Island in Canada and so we started

128
00:12:53,620 --> 00:12:59,260
 to get nice looking logos which we'd never had before and visual elements. And then we

129
00:12:59,260 --> 00:13:05,900
 tried to figure out what to do with the start page because we knew that Mozilla development

130
00:13:05,900 --> 00:13:13,380
 tools weren't the right answer and we just took us forever. And we looked at all sorts

131
00:13:13,380 --> 00:13:19,860
 of things and finally decided the one thing we knew that everybody did was search. You

132
00:13:19,860 --> 00:13:23,180
 know we'd have people who'd say well it's obvious you should have the BBC. At least

133
00:13:23,180 --> 00:13:27,160
 in English language versions it's obvious. And then you know our engineering guy would

134
00:13:27,160 --> 00:13:30,460
 look up and say well it's not obvious to my 17 year old daughter that that's what she

135
00:13:30,460 --> 00:13:36,700
 wants. It's not obvious to my son either so. But everybody used search. So we thought we

136
00:13:36,700 --> 00:13:42,860
 have the search box. Maybe we should put that on the start page too. Make it easier. Maybe

137
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:50,700
 more obvious to people about search. So we went to talk with the search providers. We

138
00:13:50,700 --> 00:13:57,860
 had a very fruitful discussion with Google. I think they also saw the value of having

139
00:13:57,860 --> 00:14:02,780
 a Mozilla browser in the world. They were very explicit about that and I believe them.

140
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:09,860
 And so they were also open to doing a business arrangement. And so often people ask how Mozilla

141
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:16,980
 gets paid and we're like most businesses on the web. Search and ads. You do a search.

142
00:14:16,980 --> 00:14:22,180
 You know you'll see ads. If you follow those ads revenue is generated. You know it goes

143
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:26,140
 through the system and we get a little piece of it. As do other browser makers. So it's

144
00:14:26,140 --> 00:14:31,540
 very similar to the business model on the web. And we did something that I believe had

145
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:37,380
 never been done before which was to make sure that Google and Yahoo were right there next

146
00:14:37,380 --> 00:14:41,420
 to each other. You don't see them next to each other. You have to click on the arrow

147
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:46,300
 but they're there. And today people laugh at me when I say that because you only see

148
00:14:46,300 --> 00:14:52,540
 one. But I negotiated that. And that was an absolute utter I will walk away from the deal

149
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:58,300
 term to everyone. Because I don't think I'd ever seen it before. And I wasn't. You know

150
00:14:58,300 --> 00:15:01,820
 what if you get on a plane and you want a Diet Coke but the plane only has Diet Pepsi

151
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:05,740
 because that's their deal. You're angry and if you want a Diet Pepsi and you want a Coke.

152
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:11,620
 And I used that example. I said I'm not going to have Firefox users angry at us because

153
00:15:11,620 --> 00:15:15,300
 they wanted one or the other. You know you have to agree. You're both going to be in

154
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:22,340
 the product. But it also is your values right. It is your values front and center. Yes because

155
00:15:22,340 --> 00:15:29,020
 the choice is there. We can do it. And to not do it would be to take away a choice that

156
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:36,220
 somebody really wants and it matters to someone. So we have that choice in the search box but

157
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:41,140
 we still didn't really know what to expect. So we shipped Firefox. And our sense that

158
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:46,820
 we were onto something turned out to be far more true even than we knew. We'd seen a rise

159
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:52,540
 in interest from 0.8 and 0.9 that was pretty noticeable but once we hit the release version

160
00:15:52,540 --> 00:15:57,700
 it just exploded. And part of that was it was the perfect product. This time we had

161
00:15:57,700 --> 00:16:02,100
 a product at the right time in the market. Internet capabilities had grown enough people

162
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:07,140
 could actually download a browser easily and more people had bandwidth and ability to do

163
00:16:07,140 --> 00:16:13,520
 so and a comfort level to try it. So that was good. We had a beautiful product and it

164
00:16:13,520 --> 00:16:19,860
 was an important product and the alternative was horrendous and dangerous and awful. And

165
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:25,660
 so all of that combined to create this giant excitement. And so Firefox market share climbed

166
00:16:25,660 --> 00:16:32,140
 by a percentage point immediately and started moving these huge numbers. So it was a completely

167
00:16:32,140 --> 00:16:37,300
 viral storm with nothing driving it other than the product and the need itself. And

168
00:16:37,300 --> 00:16:43,940
 so that took everyone by surprise. We were hoping to be able to generate a few million

169
00:16:43,940 --> 00:16:48,080
 dollars to support ourselves for the next year. We had 10 or 11 employees. You start

170
00:16:48,080 --> 00:16:53,500
 to add that up. So we were hoping to make enough money to do that. And it turned out

171
00:16:53,500 --> 00:16:57,700
 we generated that amount of money before the end of the year. The six weeks or eight weeks

172
00:16:57,700 --> 00:17:03,060
 or whatever it was before the end of the year. And so things actually things got even more

173
00:17:03,060 --> 00:17:09,340
 stressful and more hectic at that point because now you've kind of got the proverbial tiger

174
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:15,240
 by the tail. We're still 12 people. Right. So that immediate need to grow and the sense

175
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:21,580
 of pressure and stress combined with elation and success is probably, you know, I don't

176
00:17:21,580 --> 00:17:25,940
 know, maybe it's like having a child. Right. But it's that combination doesn't immediately

177
00:17:25,940 --> 00:17:30,980
 get easier. It was a little easier to be anonymous than nervous. Yes. Yes, that's for sure. We

178
00:17:30,980 --> 00:17:37,580
 certainly see that. And so by the end of Firefox shipped in 2004, just actually we just passed

179
00:17:37,580 --> 00:17:43,820
 the eight year anniversary. And by the end of 2004, it was pretty clear that we were

180
00:17:43,820 --> 00:17:49,140
 a different organization. We were, I would say, still struggling with a different set

181
00:17:49,140 --> 00:17:55,500
 of problems, but we had changed the nature of the problems really quite fundamentally.

182
00:17:55,500 --> 00:18:02,780
 And you know, we've done so in a way that that our users loved. By 2005, we were in

183
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:10,820
 a really different world where we began to be able to actually influence others. And

184
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:15,100
 that's always been the goal. I mean, market share is nice and it's nice when people love

185
00:18:15,100 --> 00:18:18,980
 your product. Market share is a validation that you produce the right thing. And so that's

186
00:18:18,980 --> 00:18:23,680
 awesome on its own. But the more important, maybe not more important, equally important

187
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:30,620
 goal is to be able to influence not only ourselves, but others in the industry so that we start

188
00:18:30,620 --> 00:18:32,140
 to see more of the things that we care about.

